{
  "reference_answer": "I'd say teamwork is useful mainly because it lets people cover each other's weak spots. Nobody's good at everything, so when you work with others, you can lean on their strengths and they can lean on yours. For example, in a group project, one person might be great at planning while another is better at actually getting things done, and together they produce something neither could manage alone. It also takes some pressure off you, because you're not carrying the whole thing by yourself. That said, teamwork only works if people actually communicate, otherwise it just turns into confusion. So for me, the real value is that it turns a bunch of individual skills into something bigger than the sum of its parts.",
  "reference_angle_summary": "独立主线：从“互补短板、分担压力”切入，用小组项目举例，并补充“沟通不到位反而添乱”的转折；示例不代表用户经历。",
