{
  "reference_answer": "For me, the real usefulness of teamwork shows up when a problem is too big or too messy for one person to even see clearly. A single person tends to get stuck in their own way of thinking, but once you put three or four heads together, someone usually spots the angle you completely missed. I noticed this when a friend's small business was losing customers — she kept blaming the product, but her staff pointed out it was actually the slow replies to messages. That's something she'd never have seen alone. So teamwork isn't just about sharing the workload; it's about catching each other's blind spots. Of course, that only works if people feel safe enough to speak up, otherwise you just get one voice and a lot of nodding.",
  "reference_angle_summary": "独立主线：团队合作的价值在于帮人看见个人视角的盲区，并以小生意案例说明；示例不代表用户经历。",
  "takeaway_expressions": [
    {
      "expression": "get stuck in their own way of thinking",
      "meaning_zh": "陷入自己固有的思维方式",
      "why_useful": "描述思维局限的高频口语搭配，可用于讨论合作、学习、决策等话题。",
      "example": "When you work alone too long, you get stuck in your own way of thinking.",
      "usage_note": "their 可替换为 my/his/her，视主语而定。"
    },
    {
      "expression": "spots the angle you completely missed",
      "meaning_zh": "发现你完全忽略的角度",
      "why_useful": "自然表达“别人看到你看不到的东西”，适合团队、沟通、创意类话题。",
      "example": "A good teammate spots the angle you completely missed.",
      "usage_note": "angle 在此指看问题的角度，不是物理角度。"
    },
    {
      "expression": "feel safe enough to speak up",
      "meaning_zh": "有足够安全感敢于开口表达",
      "why_useful": "讨论团队氛围、沟通条件时非常实用，比 simply say 更具体。",
      "example": "Teamwork only works if people feel safe enough to speak up.",
      "usage_note": "speak up 指主动说出想法，常与 safe enough 搭配。"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "reference_answer": "I'd say teamwork is useful less for what it adds and more for what it prevents. When you work alone, there's nobody to challenge your assumptions, so small mistakes can quietly grow into big ones. In a team, though, someone usually notices when something doesn't add up, and that early catch saves a lot of trouble later. I've seen this in part-time jobs — one person misses a detail, another spots it before it reaches the customer. So the real value isn't just extra hands; it's having a natural check on your own blind spots. That said, it only works if people feel comfortable speaking up. If everyone just agrees to keep the peace, you lose that safety net entirely.",
  "reference_angle_summary": "独立主线：团队合作的价值在于“预防错误”而非“增加产出”，强调早期纠错与心理安全的条件；示例不代表用户经历。",
  "takeaway_expressions": [
    {
      "expression": "doesn't add up",
      "meaning_zh": "（情况、说法）不对劲、不合逻辑",
      "why_useful": "口语中高频用来表达发现疑点或矛盾，自然且地道。",
      "example": "Something about his story doesn't add up.",
      "usage_note": "常用于描述逻辑或事实上的不一致。"
    },
    {
      "expression": "a natural check on",
      "meaning_zh": "对……的一种自然制约/检查",
      "why_useful": "可迁移到讨论制度、习惯、团队等话题，表达“制衡”概念。",
      "example": "Having a deadline is a natural check on procrastination.",
      "usage_note": "后接名词或动名词。"
    },
    {
      "expression": "keep the peace",
      "meaning_zh": "维持和睦、避免冲突",
      "why_useful": "描述人际互动时非常常用，能体现让步或妥协的语境。",
      "example": "He didn't argue just to keep the peace.",
      "usage_note": "常与 just to 连用，表示为了和谐而忍让。"
    }
  ]
}
